analysisWe need to generate assert statement for comment after offer method.

Comment: 
// @@@ If the added element is null, then the last element in the list is also null; if the added element is not null, then it equals the last element in the list.

We need to refer to last element in list. There is method getLast() that returns last element (throws if empty). After adding element, list size increased by 1, so last element should be the added element (or null). So we need to express condition: (e == null) => this.getLast() == null, and (e != null) => this.getLast().equals(e) maybe using ==? In Java spec, we can use == for reference equality. Probably they want equals. In earlier examples they used .equals for objects. For null case they used == null. So we can write:

assert (e == null) => this.getLast() == null && (e != null) => this.getLast().equals(e);

But need parentheses because => lower precedence than && . So we need: assert (e == null) => this.getLast() == null && (e != null) => this.getLast().equals(e);

But this is ambiguous: need parentheses around each implication maybe: assert (e == null) => (this.getLast() == null) && (e != null) => (this.getLast().equals(e));

But the syntax: A => B && C => D? Since && has higher priority, we need parentheses: (e == null) => (this.getLast() == null) && (e != null) => (this.getLast().equals(e))

Thus final code: <code>assert (e == null) => (this.getLast() == null) && (e != null) => (this.getLast().equals(e));</code>

Check usage of this.getLast() after add. add(e) returns boolean, but we are in offer method, which calls add(e). After that, we assert.

Make sure we only use publicly accessible observer methods: getLast() is public. So fine.

Return value \result not needed.

