The comment: 
"@@@ If the added element is null, then the last element in the list is also null; if the added element is not null, then it equals the last element in the list."

We need to express this as an assert after the method body (which calls add(e) and returns its result). The method returns boolean, but the comment is about the last element in the list after adding e.

We have observer methods: probably getLast() returns the last element, size(), etc. We can use this.getLast().

We need to refer to the added element e (parameter). Need to express conditional: (e == null) => this.getLast() == null, and (e != null) => this.getLast().equals(e). Use Java equality: for objects we can use == for null, and .equals for non-null.

Spec: assert (e == null) => this.getLast() == null && (e != null) => this.getLast().equals(e);

But need proper parentheses: (e == null) => this.getLast() == null && (e != null) => this.getLast().equals(e). However precedence: => lower than &&, so we need parentheses around each implication or combine with &&.

Better: assert (e == null) => this.getLast() == null && (e != null) => this.getLast().equals(e);

But need parentheses around each implication? Not necessary because => lower priority, so (e == null) => this.getLast() == null && (e != null) => this.getLast().equals(e) would parse as ((e == null) => (this.getLast() == null && (e != null) => this.getLast().equals(e))) which is wrong. So we need parentheses: assert (e == null) => (this.getLast() == null) && (e != null) => (this.getLast().equals(e));

But still precedence: && higher than =>, so we need to separate: assert ((e == null) => (this.getLast() == null)) && ((e != null) => (this.getLast().equals(e)));

Yes.

Thus final assert:

<code>assert ((e == null) => (this.getLast() == null)) && ((e != null) => (this.getLast().equals(e)));</code>

